Q: how to select matching rows in multiple files with AWK A: <p>With any POSIX <code>awk</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$ cat foo.awk
BEGIN { for(i = 1; i &lt; ARGC; i++) s0 = s0 &quot;\t&quot; }

function insert(s, v, p) {
  if(! s) s = s0
  match(s, &quot;^([^&quot; FS &quot;]*&quot; FS &quot;){&quot; p - 1 &quot;}&quot;)
  return substr(s, 1, RLENGTH) v substr(s, RLENGTH + 1)
}

FNR==1 { header = insert(header, FILENAME, ++nf) }

{
  line[$2] = insert(line[$2], $1, nf)
  cnt[$2] += 1
}

END {
  for(m in cnt) if(cnt[m] != nf) delete line[m]
  if(length(line)) print insert(header, &quot;M-pattern&quot;, nf + 1)
  for(m in line) print insert(line[m], m, nf + 1)
}

$ awk -f foo.awk -F'\t' file1 file2 file3
file1   file2   file3   M-pattern
79      72      73      M10
8191    8584    9090    M0
139559  119837  137373  M1
</code></pre>
<p>Explanations:</p>
<p>Let <code>N</code> be the number of files passed to <code>awk</code> (3 in your example). The <code>insert</code> function takes 3 parameters:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>A string <code>s</code> which is a tab-separated concatenation of <code>N+1</code> fields (if <code>s</code> is empty <code>insert</code> first initializes it with the <code>s0</code> string computed in the <code>BEGIN</code> block, that contains only <code>N</code> tabs, that is, <code>N+1</code> empty fields separated by <code>N</code> tabs).</p>
</li>
<li><p>A field value <code>v</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The position <code>p</code> of an empty field, between 1 for the leftmost field and <code>N+1</code> for the rightmost field.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Function <code>insert</code> replaces empty field number <code>p</code> in <code>s</code> with <code>v</code> and returns the result. We use function <code>insert</code> to prepare the header line (variable <code>header</code>) and the other output lines (array <code>line</code> indexed by the M-patterns), that we print in the <code>END</code> block.</p>
<p>Array <code>cnt</code> is used to print only the M-patterns found in all files and to print the header line only if at least one M-pattern was found in all files.</p>

